1,8,2,1,2,6,1,5,8,1,8,9,1,2,5],[8,1,2,6,1,2,2,1,2,3,1,2,5,1,2,9,1,2,8,1,2,6,1,2,2,1,2,3,1],[1,8,2,1,5,9,1,2,2,1,8,9,1,5,2,1,2,9,1,8,2,1,5,9,1,2,2,1,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid is a wallpaper: one small motif repeats across the whole canvas. A few solid blocks of colour 0 cover parts of it, and the output is the same wallpaper with those blocks filled back in.\n\n**What the examples show**\n- Outside the 0 blocks, input and output are identical. In every pair, the only cells that change are 0 cells (the fact sheet lists only `0 -> something` transitions). Colour 0 never appears in the output.\n- The patterns are tilings, but they are not always aligned to the rows. In pair 1, each row is the row above shifted sideways. In pair 2 the period is 3 rows by 6 columns. In pair 3 the motif repeats every 2 rows and 8 columns, again with a diagonal shift.\n- So the right tool is translation symmetry rather than a fixed rectangular tile. A shift (dy, dx) is a symmetry of the pattern if every pair of known (non-0) cells that lie (dy, dx) apart have the same colour.\n\n**Construction**\n1. Treat colour 0 as unknown. It is the mask colour in every pair, so a constant is justified.\n2. Try every shift with 0 ≤ dy < H and −W < dx < W.\n   - Keep a shift only if its overlapping known cells all agree.\n   - Also require a decent overlap (at least 40 compared cells), so that a shift with only a few overlapping cells cannot pass by chance.\n   - Sort the surviving shifts from shortest to longest.\n3. Fill each 0 block (one connected component of 0s) at a time. For each hidden cell, step from it by multiples of each valid shift, in both directions. Take the first known colour found.\n4. If a cell still cannot be resolved, repeat the pass, now using cells filled earlier. This is a safety net.\n\n**Emitted stages**\n- Each repaired 0 block is emitted as its own stage.\n- The finished grid is emitted at the end.\n\n**The query input**\n- The test grid has four 0 blocks over a periodic pattern on background 1. Each block is restored by copying the colours found at a valid repeat shift.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    mask_col = 0\n    known = g != mask_col\n    # find valid translation symmetries\n    offs = []\n    for dy in range(0, H):\n        for dx in range(-W + 1, W):\n            if dy == 0 and dx <= 0:\n                continue\n            y0, y1 = 0, H - dy\n            x0, x1 = max(0, -dx), min(W, W - dx)\n            if y1 <= y0 or x1 <= x0:\n                continue\n            a = g[y0:y1, x0:x1]\n            b = g[y0 + dy:y1 + dy, x0 + dx:x1 + dx]\n            m = known[y0:y1, x0:x1] & known[y0 + dy:y1 + dy, x0 + dx:x1 + dx]\n            n = m.sum()\n            if n >= 40 and np.all(a[m] == b[m]):\n                offs.append((dy, dx))\n    offs.sort(key=lambda o: o[0] ** 2 + o[1] ** 2)\n\n    def lookup(y, x):\n        for dy, dx in offs:\n            for s in (1, -1):\n                k = 1\n                while True:\n                    yy, xx = y + s * k * dy, x + s * k * dx\n                    if not (0 <= yy < H and 0 <= xx < W):\n                        break\n                    if g[yy, xx] != mask_col:\n                        return g[yy, xx]\n                    k += 1\n        return None\n\n    comps = components(g == mask_col)\n    for comp in comps:\n        for (y, x) in comp:\n            v = lookup(y, x)\n            if v is not None:\n                g[y, x] = v\n        emit(g, f\"Restore the masked block at {bbox(comp)} by copying the colours found at a repeat offset of the periodic pattern.\")\n    # safety passes for any unresolved cells\n    for _ in range(5):\n        rem = list(zip(*np.where(g == mask_col)))\n        if not rem:\n            break\n        for (y, x) in rem:\n            v = lookup(y, x)\n            if v is not None:\n                g[y, x] = v\n    emit(g, \"All masked cells are filled; the periodic wallpaper is complete.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Fill each 0 block (one connected component of 0s) at a time."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Sort the surviving shifts from shortest to longest."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- So the right tool is translation symmetry rather than a fixed rectangular tile."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"A shift (dy, dx) is a symmetry of the pattern if every pair of known (non-0) cells that lie (dy, dx) apart have the same colour."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In pair 1, each row is the row above shifted sideways."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In pair 3 the motif repeats every 2 rows and 8 columns, again with a diagonal shift."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- So the right tool is translation symmetry rather than a fixed rectangular tile."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In pair 2 the period is 3 rows by 6 columns."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- So the right tool is translation symmetry rather than a fixed rectangular tile."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The test grid has four 0 blocks over a periodic pattern on background 1."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The grid is a wallpaper: one small motif repeats across the whole canvas."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In pair 3 the motif repeats every 2 rows and 8 columns, again with a diagonal shift."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each block is restored by copying the colours found at a valid repeat shift."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Restore the masked block at (1, 0, 4, 3) by copying the colours found at a repeat offset of the periodic pattern."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Restore the masked block at (11, 19, 16, 23) by copying the colours found at a repeat offset of the periodic pattern."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Sort the surviving shifts from shortest to longest."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The patterns are tilings, but they are not always aligned to the rows."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Keep a shift only if its overlapping known cells all agree."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Also require a decent overlap (at least 40 compared cells), so that a shift with only a few overlapping cells cannot pass by chance."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c76d1723e11731f584bd79fab18d7234f207ee644696acf906d2c9fd60bd746f","task_sha256":"e8857f276420715f98feda96fb05fb49997440814212b987a291821f6dea280e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
